# {{ ansible_managed }}
#
# The box's own copy of the deployment's identity — the same six values the operator's `.env`
# carries on their machine, in the same `KEY=value` shape, so the same one line reads it:
#
#     set -a; . /etc/jamground/deploy.env; set +a
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. A deploy runs on this box, not on the operator's machine:
# jamground-deploy runs `npm run build`, and that build resolves jamground.config.mjs out of
# whatever environment it is handed. The operator's `.env` is on the operator's laptop and
# Ansible copies it nowhere, so without this file the build resolves all six to their committed
# placeholders and emits `example.com` canonical URLs — on a box that is serving production, in
# a build that exits 0. The converge is the one moment that knows both the operator's
# environment and the box, so the converge is what writes this down.
#
# WHAT MAY BE IN HERE. Exactly the six values group_vars/all.yml declares, which are exactly the
# six jamground.config.mjs reads. Each is a property of the FORK rather than of a person, and
# each is PUBLIC by construction: all six are baked into editor/dist/shell.js at build time
# wherever they are written, so there is nothing here a stricter mode could hide. That is why
# this file is 0644 and root-owned — readable by the unprivileged build account, writable by
# nobody but the converge.
#
# WHAT NEVER MAY. NO SECRET, EVER. Not the OAuth client secret, not the bot PAT, not the ACME
# credentials. Those three are named by PATH in .env.example, they live at those paths on this
# box with the modes their own services require, and each is read by the one service that needs
# it. A secret written here would be world-readable on a box that runs an unprivileged build
# account and a webhook receiver. test/gates/deploy-env-names.test.mjs asserts that this
# template names the six and nothing else, so "never" is checked rather than promised.
#
# Values reach the box, not Jinja: this renders once per converge, from the environment the
# operator sourced their `.env` into. Unquoted `KEY=value` is safe for these six in particular —
# a hostname, two GitHub names, an org, a branch name and a client id, none of which can contain
# whitespace or a shell metacharacter — and it keeps this file the same shape as `.env`, which is
# what lets one documented idiom read both.
#
# A fork that has not converged this role yet has no file here at all. jamground-deploy is
# written to survive that: it sources this only if present, and a box without it builds the
# placeholders rather than refusing to deploy.
JAMGROUND_DOMAIN={{ jamground_domain }}
JAMGROUND_GITHUB_ORG={{ jamground_github_org }}
JAMGROUND_SITE_REPO={{ jamground_site_repo }}
JAMGROUND_CONTENT_REPO={{ jamground_content_repo }}
JAMGROUND_CONTENT_BRANCH={{ jamground_content_branch }}
JAMGROUND_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID={{ jamground_oauth_client_id }}
